1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,565
We study how AI recommendations change access to
opportunities with limited capacity.

2
00:00:06,745 --> 00:00:10,933
A useful list for one person can change someone else's
outcome.

3
00:00:11,113 --> 00:00:16,549
Our question is simple: when everyone follows the
recommendations, who gets a place?

4
00:00:18,350 --> 00:00:21,133
Start with two applicants and two school seats.

5
00:00:21,313 --> 00:00:24,023
Applicant A prefers school x to school y.

6
00:00:24,203 --> 00:00:26,558
Applicant B accepts only x.

7
00:00:26,738 --> 00:00:29,149
School x gives A priority over B.

8
00:00:29,329 --> 00:00:32,193
Initially, A is shown y and B is shown x.

9
00:00:32,373 --> 00:00:34,571
The blue lines show their assignments.

10
00:00:34,751 --> 00:00:36,031
Both get a seat.

11
00:00:36,211 --> 00:00:38,856
Now change only A's recommendation.

12
00:00:39,036 --> 00:00:40,913
A sees x instead of y.

13
00:00:41,093 --> 00:00:43,803
Both applicants still see exactly one school.

14
00:00:43,983 --> 00:00:47,565
But they now compete for x, and A has higher priority.

15
00:00:47,745 --> 00:00:51,261
A gets x, B gets no seat, and y stays empty.

16
00:00:51,441 --> 00:00:53,264
The new suggestion helps A.

17
00:00:53,444 --> 00:00:57,300
B's own list has not changed at all, but B loses
access.

18
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:01,916
That is the basic reason to evaluate recommendations
jointly.

19
00:01:02,096 --> 00:01:06,723
A person's outcome depends on what other people are
encouraged to pursue.

20
00:01:06,903 --> 00:01:10,240
This can happen in housing and jobs as well as
schools.

21
00:01:11,350 --> 00:01:14,857
We separate the recommendations from the assignment
rule.

22
00:01:15,037 --> 00:01:20,483
First, the recommender chooses a shortlist of feasible
options for each person.

23
00:01:20,663 --> 00:01:23,916
Then the assignment rule decides who gets which place.

24
00:01:24,096 --> 00:01:25,747
We measure three things.

25
00:01:25,927 --> 00:01:28,187
A match means someone receives a place.

26
00:01:28,367 --> 00:01:33,483
Target coverage means a particular reference choice
appears on their shortlist.

27
00:01:33,663 --> 00:01:40,442
A lost match means someone receives a place under one
policy, but not under the policy we compare it with.

28
00:01:40,622 --> 00:01:43,106
The reference differs across our studies.

29
00:01:43,286 --> 00:01:49,044
In rentals it is the simulated assignment when all
feasible options are available.

30
00:01:49,224 --> 00:01:52,943
In Chile it is the school recorded in later
enrollment.

31
00:01:53,123 --> 00:01:59,284
Our question is whether recommendations can improve
while everyone who had a place stays matched
somewhere.

32
00:01:59,464 --> 00:02:00,925
They may switch schools.

33
00:02:01,105 --> 00:02:05,113
Keeping a seat does not necessarily mean getting a
better school.

34
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:08,121
Here is our clearest finding.

35
00:02:08,301 --> 00:02:14,152
In the rental simulation, focus on renters with only
one to five feasible apartments.

36
00:02:14,332 --> 00:02:17,499
Every policy already shows them all their options.

37
00:02:17,679 --> 00:02:28,277
Their match rate rises from 13.1 percent to 27.5
percent when we change how recommendations are
coordinated across the market.

38
00:02:28,457 --> 00:02:29,950
That is more than double.

39
00:02:30,130 --> 00:02:32,967
Yet their own shortlists stay exactly the same.

40
00:02:33,147 --> 00:02:35,579
Only other renters' shortlists change.

41
00:02:35,759 --> 00:02:41,770
We compare a policy coordinating one option with a
policy balancing the whole shortlist.

42
00:02:41,950 --> 00:02:43,784
I will explain both shortly.

43
00:02:43,964 --> 00:02:50,114
The result shows how much competition can matter even
when your own recommendations never change.

44
00:02:50,294 --> 00:02:54,268
It is a simulation result, not measured housing
outcomes.

45
00:02:56,350 --> 00:02:58,526
The settings serve different purposes.

46
00:02:58,706 --> 00:03:05,570
Rentals let us study actual saved GPT recommendations
and compare ways to coordinate them.

47
00:03:05,750 --> 00:03:15,955
Listings and household profiles come from market and
survey data, but renter preferences, landlord
priorities, and resulting assignments are modeled.

48
00:03:16,135 --> 00:03:24,833
We reconstruct 1,280 markets and verify all 55,115
cached candidate hashes.

49
00:03:25,013 --> 00:03:29,296
These new comparisons use previously analyzed calendar
windows.

50
00:03:29,476 --> 00:03:36,938
Some homes recur across them, so this is a follow-up
analysis, not independent property-level validation.

51
00:03:37,118 --> 00:03:41,828
Chile gives us families' submitted rankings and the
actual admissions rules.

52
00:03:42,008 --> 00:03:49,490
Our reconstruction matches the released course or no-
offer outcomes in about two million audited applicant-
years.

53
00:03:49,670 --> 00:03:51,889
That checks institutional fidelity.

54
00:03:52,069 --> 00:03:56,970
The new shortlists still produce counterfactual
outcomes under fixed reports.

55
00:03:57,150 --> 00:04:05,648
The connection across settings is competition for
limited capacity, not an assumption that housing and
schools behave identically.

56
00:04:07,350 --> 00:04:10,209
We test two simple coordination approaches.

57
00:04:10,389 --> 00:04:15,024
The anchor first computes a maximum matching on all
feasible pairs.

58
00:04:15,204 --> 00:04:20,758
It prefers pairs already on the shortlists when
several maximum matchings are available.

59
00:04:20,938 --> 00:04:27,963
Each assigned option is then inserted into its
recipient's shortlist, replacing at most one option.

60
00:04:28,143 --> 00:04:29,764
List lengths stay fixed.

61
00:04:29,944 --> 00:04:32,258
Balancing takes a broader approach.

62
00:04:32,438 --> 00:04:35,616
Count how many times each listing is recommended.

63
00:04:35,796 --> 00:04:42,010
Minimize the sum of those counts squared, which
discourages everyone from seeing the same few
listings.

64
00:04:42,190 --> 00:04:48,052
Among equally balanced shortlists, favor options
higher in the saved GPT ranking.

65
00:04:48,232 --> 00:04:51,046
We solve this exactly with min-cost flow.

66
00:04:51,226 --> 00:04:55,542
Both policies use feasible pairs and saved
recommendations.

67
00:04:55,722 --> 00:05:01,701
Neither sees the realized preference shocks, landlord
priorities, or reference assignments.

68
00:05:01,881 --> 00:05:05,048
Balancing can replace several options per person.

69
00:05:05,228 --> 00:05:12,116
It is a stronger coordination control, not an equal-
edit-budget comparison with the one-option anchor.

70
00:05:12,296 --> 00:05:16,370
Neither approach imposes an individual retention
constraint.

71
00:05:18,350 --> 00:05:23,393
First look at the original comparison with random
shortlists of the same length.

72
00:05:23,573 --> 00:05:25,845
Blue is reference-option coverage.

73
00:05:26,025 --> 00:05:27,539
Orange is placement.

74
00:05:27,719 --> 00:05:30,087
Zero means no difference from random.

75
00:05:30,267 --> 00:05:37,622
Across all three enriched calendar windows, GPT
increases coverage by about eight percentage points.

76
00:05:37,802 --> 00:05:40,490
Placement falls by about two to four points.

77
00:05:40,670 --> 00:05:45,755
The directions repeat, and the original market-
bootstrap intervals exclude zero.

78
00:05:45,935 --> 00:05:50,497
This does not mean GPT makes worse predictions about
everything.

79
00:05:50,677 --> 00:05:59,804
It means that covering an individual's full-list
assignment does not determine how many people can be
placed when everyone's shortlists interact.

80
00:05:59,984 --> 00:06:05,932
These markets deliberately include affordable listings
and renters with feasible options.

81
00:06:06,112 --> 00:06:11,579
The ordinary sampling design and all uncertainty
details are in the appendix.

82
00:06:13,350 --> 00:06:16,048
Now put four policies on the same axes.

83
00:06:16,228 --> 00:06:18,936
Farther right means more reference coverage.

84
00:06:19,116 --> 00:06:21,164
Higher means more people placed.

85
00:06:21,344 --> 00:06:23,509
Random shortlists are on the left.

86
00:06:23,689 --> 00:06:26,493
GPT moves right, but slightly down.

87
00:06:26,673 --> 00:06:33,250
The one-option anchor moves GPT upward, recovering
placement with a small change in coverage.

88
00:06:33,430 --> 00:06:35,563
Balanced shortlists go much higher.

89
00:06:35,743 --> 00:06:44,837
Relative to the anchor, they gain another 12.6
percentage points of placement while losing about
three points of reference coverage in this panel.

90
00:06:45,017 --> 00:06:46,604
The comparison matters.

91
00:06:46,784 --> 00:06:50,918
Relative to plain random, balancing improves both
outcomes.

92
00:06:51,098 --> 00:06:56,598
So the evidence does not show an unavoidable conflict
between coverage and placement.

93
00:06:56,778 --> 00:07:00,764
These are point means for the September and October
enriched panel.

94
00:07:00,944 --> 00:07:06,584
A control using extra information from the simulation
improves both measures further.

95
00:07:06,764 --> 00:07:10,524
Detailed controls and paired intervals are in the
appendix.

96
00:07:10,704 --> 00:07:14,510
We have not established GPT as the best recommender.

97
00:07:14,690 --> 00:07:18,913
This plot also cannot tell us whether any individual
is protected.

98
00:07:20,350 --> 00:07:27,481
Return to the anchor and compare it with the original
GPT shortlists on exactly the same markets.

99
00:07:27,661 --> 00:07:31,806
There are 449 newly placed renter appearances.

100
00:07:31,986 --> 00:07:37,519
There are also 37 exits: people who were placed before
and are now unplaced.

101
00:07:37,699 --> 00:07:42,665
Subtracting the exits from the entrants gives the 412
net gain.

102
00:07:42,845 --> 00:07:46,658
Reporting only that net gain would hide the loss of
access.

103
00:07:46,838 --> 00:07:50,408
The more effective balancing policy has the same
issue.

104
00:07:50,588 --> 00:07:57,356
Compared with the anchor, it creates many more
placements but also causes 171 exits.

105
00:07:57,536 --> 00:08:01,128
An exit is defined relative to a named policy.

106
00:08:01,308 --> 00:08:06,488
These counts describe repeated simulated appearances,
not unique households.

107
00:08:06,668 --> 00:08:13,181
Their role is to test an individual protection claim
that an average placement rate cannot establish.

108
00:08:14,350 --> 00:08:20,479
The cleanest competition result concerns renters with
only one to five feasible listings.

109
00:08:20,659 --> 00:08:25,520
Our policies show up to five options, so these renters
already see everything.

110
00:08:25,700 --> 00:08:28,068
Their personal shortlists cannot change.

111
00:08:28,248 --> 00:08:33,919
When the whole market uses the anchor, this group's
placement rate is 13.1 percent.

112
00:08:34,099 --> 00:08:37,616
Under balancing, it is 27.5 percent.

113
00:08:37,796 --> 00:08:44,043
That is a 14.4-point gain even though the group's own
recommendations are identical.

114
00:08:44,223 --> 00:08:46,942
The change comes from other renters' shortlists.

115
00:08:47,122 --> 00:08:51,514
Coordinating those shortlists changes who competes for
each listing.

116
00:08:51,694 --> 00:08:55,414
This is a sizable positive spillover inside the model.

117
00:08:55,594 --> 00:09:03,560
It still does not protect every member of the group:
15 previously placed appearances lose access under
balancing.

118
00:09:03,740 --> 00:09:08,632
Better group outcomes and zero individual losses
remain different claims.

119
00:09:10,350 --> 00:09:15,039
Rentals give us controlled comparisons, but their
preferences are modeled.

120
00:09:15,219 --> 00:09:23,726
Chile lets us examine the same access question using
families' submitted rankings and a reconstruction of
the admissions rules.

121
00:09:23,906 --> 00:09:29,373
We compare two classifiers trained on an earlier
cohort to predict initial offers.

122
00:09:29,553 --> 00:09:32,601
One uses submitted rank, and the other does not.

123
00:09:32,781 --> 00:09:35,576
They select short shortlists for later cohorts.

124
00:09:35,756 --> 00:09:42,332
We score coverage against later observed enrollment
and replay admissions on the selected options.

125
00:09:42,512 --> 00:09:54,880
The rank-informed policy gains 4,638 enrollment hits
overall, but loses 1,670 placements relative to rank-
blind shortlists.

126
00:09:55,060 --> 00:10:00,040
The graph separates pupils by whether they
historically received an initial offer.

127
00:10:00,220 --> 00:10:03,014
Each bar uses its own group's denominator.

128
00:10:03,194 --> 00:10:12,426
Pupils with no initial offer make up 7.4 percent of
the linked sample but account for 76 percent of the
net placement loss.

129
00:10:12,606 --> 00:10:17,786
That is 1,270 net lost placements in the smaller
group.

130
00:10:17,966 --> 00:10:24,863
Its placement rate falls by 0.873 percentage points in
the counterfactual comparison.

131
00:10:25,043 --> 00:10:27,431
These are not revoked official offers.

132
00:10:27,611 --> 00:10:36,534
Historical offer status defines the groups; placement
loss compares two new shortlist policies under fixed
reported preferences.

133
00:10:36,714 --> 00:10:41,128
The concentration of the loss is what motivates the
protection question.

134
00:10:42,350 --> 00:10:44,707
There is an important check on that result.

135
00:10:44,887 --> 00:10:51,709
Compare the same rank-informed policy with each
family's first five discretionary courses instead.

136
00:10:51,889 --> 00:11:00,696
Now it improves both aggregates: 9,800 more enrollment
hits and 8,143 more placements.

137
00:11:00,876 --> 00:11:04,799
Again, a prediction–placement conflict is not
inevitable.

138
00:11:04,979 --> 00:11:14,573
But the group without a historical initial offer still
loses 2,860 counterfactual placements against this
alternative.

139
00:11:14,753 --> 00:11:20,894
That is why the comparison policy and the affected
population both need to be visible.

140
00:11:21,074 --> 00:11:26,435
An overall improvement can coexist with worse access
for a particular group.

141
00:11:26,615 --> 00:11:35,358
We should not treat the historical enrollment target
as welfare, or assume that a larger total resolves the
distributional question.

142
00:11:37,350 --> 00:11:40,081
Here is the mathematical design problem.

143
00:11:40,261 --> 00:11:44,577
Fix the baseline shortlists and a supplied set of
priority draws.

144
00:11:44,757 --> 00:11:48,936
Choose one new shortlist policy that works across
every draw.

145
00:11:49,116 --> 00:11:55,863
Maximize extra reference hits, subject to every
baseline recipient keeping a seat on every draw.

146
00:11:56,043 --> 00:11:59,741
Recipients may switch schools, but cannot become
unplaced.

147
00:11:59,921 --> 00:12:09,614
In Chile, we start from rank-blind shortlists,
preserve options shared with rank-informed shortlists,
fill from their union, and keep each quota.

148
00:12:09,794 --> 00:12:19,250
Our theorem says that deciding whether a target gain
is achievable is NP-complete, even with unit seats and
shortlists of length at most three.

149
00:12:19,430 --> 00:12:24,184
Checking a chosen policy with deferred acceptance
remains polynomial.

150
00:12:24,364 --> 00:12:28,574
The theorem uses fixed, strict priorities on an
explicit panel.

151
00:12:28,754 --> 00:12:31,890
Chile's family-linked rules are tested separately.

152
00:12:32,070 --> 00:12:36,951
The difficulty is choosing compatible edits, as the
next slide illustrates.

153
00:12:38,350 --> 00:12:40,128
The proof starts with a graph.

154
00:12:40,308 --> 00:12:43,827
Each vertex represents a beneficial shortlist edit.

155
00:12:44,007 --> 00:12:49,167
Each edge creates one priority draw where the two
endpoint edits can conflict.

156
00:12:49,347 --> 00:12:50,819
Look at this one draw.

157
00:12:50,999 --> 00:12:56,510
Selecting edit u displaces another, already-matched
pupil toward the fallback seat.

158
00:12:56,690 --> 00:13:02,052
Selecting edit v displaces a different already-matched
pupil toward the same seat.

159
00:13:02,232 --> 00:13:04,032
Either edit alone is safe.

160
00:13:04,212 --> 00:13:07,156
Select both, and one pupil must lose a seat.

161
00:13:07,336 --> 00:13:10,238
Other vertex edits are harmless on this draw.

162
00:13:10,418 --> 00:13:12,433
We create a draw for every edge.

163
00:13:12,613 --> 00:13:15,692
One fixed set of edits must work on all of them.

164
00:13:15,872 --> 00:13:19,476
So a safe set cannot contain both endpoints of any
edge.

165
00:13:19,656 --> 00:13:21,564
That is an independent set.

166
00:13:21,744 --> 00:13:24,814
Each selected vertex adds one reference hit.

167
00:13:24,994 --> 00:13:29,610
Maximizing safe gains therefore solves the Independent
Set problem.

168
00:13:29,790 --> 00:13:32,370
The appendix gives the full construction.

169
00:13:34,350 --> 00:13:46,304
We then compare two protection requirements on the
same Chilean design panels: 1,024 lotteries per cohort
for 2022 through 2024.

170
00:13:46,484 --> 00:13:59,057
If every baseline recipient must keep the same course
and seat type, the maximum additional enrollment
coverage is exactly 314 hits within our allowed
shortlist family.

171
00:13:59,237 --> 00:14:05,994
If recipients may switch schools while keeping a seat,
we construct shortlists gaining 642 hits.

172
00:14:06,174 --> 00:14:09,980
The certified upper bound is 4,620.

173
00:14:10,160 --> 00:14:15,404
Allowing switches therefore permits more than twice
the demonstrated coverage gain.

174
00:14:15,584 --> 00:14:17,172
But the bracket is wide.

175
00:14:17,352 --> 00:14:20,296
We have not found the optimal retention policy.

176
00:14:20,476 --> 00:14:27,447
These are hindsight optimization benchmarks, using the
evaluation cohorts' later enrollment labels.

177
00:14:27,627 --> 00:14:33,660
They do not describe a policy that could choose its
shortlists before those outcomes existed.

178
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:41,111
To make the constructive answer useful, we also need a
rule based only on information available before
assignment.

179
00:14:42,350 --> 00:14:48,245
We evaluate two rules frozen after applications
arrive, before the ordinary assignment.

180
00:14:48,425 --> 00:14:54,106
Neither uses that cohort's realized offers or later
enrollment to choose shortlists.

181
00:14:54,286 --> 00:15:00,449
The structural rule protects a set of interactions
around applicants without a guaranteed fallback.

182
00:15:00,629 --> 00:15:07,632
Its proof covers all permitted ordinary lottery orders
under the fixed inputs and reconstructed mechanism.

183
00:15:07,812 --> 00:15:12,608
It gains 28 enrollment hits and has zero exits in the
test panels.

184
00:15:12,788 --> 00:15:17,423
A learned rule passes its design-panel checks and
gains 50 hits.

185
00:15:17,603 --> 00:15:21,706
But it causes an exit in each of two fresh lottery
generators.

186
00:15:21,886 --> 00:15:23,721
The guaranteed gain is small.

187
00:15:23,901 --> 00:15:32,226
The useful distinction is between a proof covering the
allowed orders and a policy that merely passed many
simulated draws.

188
00:15:33,350 --> 00:15:38,583
The social objective cannot stop with protecting
people who already have a place.

189
00:15:38,763 --> 00:15:44,614
Our structural Chile rule produces no new entrants on
the reported paired test lotteries.

190
00:15:44,794 --> 00:15:51,146
It preserves incumbents, but does not expand access
for previously unplaced pupils in those tests.

191
00:15:51,326 --> 00:15:53,948
That raises a substantive design choice.

192
00:15:54,128 --> 00:16:00,890
How should we balance protection for existing
recipients against opportunities for people who start
excluded?

193
00:16:01,070 --> 00:16:03,490
Our measurements are incomplete answers.

194
00:16:03,670 --> 00:16:07,848
Historical enrollment reflects constraints as well as
preference.

195
00:16:08,028 --> 00:16:14,392
Receiving any seat can mean moving to a worse school,
and modeled ranks are not measured household welfare.

196
00:16:14,572 --> 00:16:22,345
Before deployment, people should retain the ability to
add choices, correct errors, and seek human review or
appeal.

197
00:16:22,525 --> 00:16:26,608
Those are proposed safeguards, not interventions we
have tested.

198
00:16:26,788 --> 00:16:30,592
Recommendations redistribute existing opportunities.

199
00:16:30,772 --> 00:16:34,886
They cannot create affordable apartments or new school
capacity.

200
00:16:36,350 --> 00:16:39,931
Recommendations need a market-level evaluation.

201
00:16:40,111 --> 00:16:43,777
Coordination can improve both coverage and placement.

202
00:16:43,957 --> 00:16:48,251
But better totals do not guarantee that each person
keeps access.

203
00:16:48,431 --> 00:16:56,159
We measure that distinction, formalize the retention
problem, and test the gap between a finite test panel
and a guarantee.

204
00:16:56,339 --> 00:17:03,482
The next design problem is to improve access for
people without a place while protecting those who
already have one.

205
00:17:03,662 --> 00:17:04,686
Thank you.

